    <h1 class="hero-title">Settings Platform</h1>
    <p class="hero-subtitle">
      Redesigning the configuration experience for enterprise observability.
      From identity crisis to control tower&mdash;a 2-year vision for how teams
      configure, override, and govern platform settings at scale.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-meta">
      <div class="meta-item">
        <div class="meta-label">Company</div>
        <div class="meta-value">Dynatrace</div>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-item">
        <div class="meta-label">Role</div>
        <div class="meta-value">Lead Product Designer</div>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-item">
        <div class="meta-label">Duration</div>
        <div class="meta-value">6 Months</div>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-item">
        <div class="meta-label">Parallel With</div>
        <div class="meta-value">Spaces Initiative</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 — THE PROBLEM -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">01 &mdash; THE PROBLEM</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">An Identity Crisis at Scale</h2>
      <p class="section-description">
        The Settings Platform is the app that provides the configurable widget and canvas
        for every app inside Dynatrace. It&rsquo;s the tone setter for settings structure&mdash;a
        place where all apps render their respective settings. When I joined, the team was
        migrating from Gen 2 to Gen 3, but they weren&rsquo;t just carrying over usability
        problems&mdash;they were creating new ones.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Identity Crisis diagram -->
    <div class="research-image">
      <img src="/images/platform settings/SCR-20260406-oarw.png" alt="Identity Crisis Component analysis — the scope selector trying to serve multiple purposes">
      <p class="image-caption">The scope selector component&rsquo;s identity crisis: trying to serve navigation, scoping, and entity search simultaneously</p>
    </div>

    <div class="pain-grid">
      <div class="pain-card">
        <h4>No Landing Page</h4>
        <p>Users dropped into settings with no orientation. No search hero, no recent activity, no way to understand what needed attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pain-card">
        <h4>Identity Crisis Selector</h4>
        <p>One component tried to handle environment scoping, entity search, and navigation. Users couldn&rsquo;t tell what it was for.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pain-card">
        <h4>Wasted Visual Estate</h4>
        <p>A second sidebar consumed precious screen width. Most users were in India on small screens&mdash;nobody was tackling this.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pain-card">
        <h4>3 Competing Search Systems</h4>
        <p>Global search, settings search, and entity search all coexisted. Users defaulted to entity search because that was their actual job to be done.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pain-card">
        <h4>Broken Breadcrumbs</h4>
        <p>Breadcrumbs would disappear mid-journey because rendered canvas content from other teams didn&rsquo;t implement them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pain-card">
        <h4>No Override Visibility</h4>
        <p>Users couldn&rsquo;t see what was customized where. Entity-level overrides were buried behind &ldquo;...&rdquo; menus in individual schemas.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 — RESEARCH -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">02 &mdash; RESEARCH &amp; ANALYSIS</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Understanding the Landscape</h2>
      <p class="section-description">
        I studied 15+ platforms to understand how the best products handle settings,
        inheritance, and configuration at scale. The insight that changed everything
        wasn&rsquo;t about UI patterns&mdash;it was about culture.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Cross-platform comparison -->
    <div class="research-image">
      <img src="/images/platform settings/SCR-20260406-ocar.png" alt="Cross-platform comparison of 15+ settings experiences">
      <p class="image-caption">Competitive analysis across GitHub, Grafana, Salesforce, Terraform, AWS, Azure, Stripe, Datadog, and more</p>
    </div>

    <div class="quote-section">
      <blockquote class="large-quote">
        &ldquo;The word &lsquo;consistency&rsquo; was harming the experience. Designers focused
        only on visual consistency, generating patterns unique to Dynatrace that confused
        everyone. Consistency is multi-dimensional: internal, external, functional, and visual.
        Three of them need to be at play at all times.&rdquo;
      </blockquote>
      <cite class="quote-author">&mdash; Nilson Gaspar</cite>
    </div>

    <!-- Key problems observed -->
    <div class="research-image">
      <img src="/images/platform settings/SCR-20260406-obkg.png" alt="Key problems observed in Platform Settings — problem mapping">
      <p class="image-caption">Systematic mapping of every problem in the existing Settings experience: no hub, identity crisis, duplication, mixed paradigms</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 — THE INSIGHT -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">03 &mdash; THE INSIGHT</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Settings is a Launchpad, Not a Dashboard</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="split-section">
      <div class="split-content">
        <h3>Search-First Philosophy</h3>
        <p>
          Users come to Settings to configure something specific. They don&rsquo;t come to monitor
          or browse. The primary navigation method should be search&mdash;prominent, center-placed,
          impossible to miss.
        </p>
        <p>
          I mapped four user intents to four UI elements: &ldquo;I know what I need&rdquo; &rarr; Search bar.
          &ldquo;I was just working on...&rdquo; &rarr; Recent Activity. &ldquo;What&rsquo;s new?&rdquo; &rarr;
          What&rsquo;s New section. &ldquo;I want to browse&rdquo; &rarr; Horizontal navigation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="research-image" style="margin: 0;">
        <img src="/images/platform settings/SCR-20260406-obmw.png" alt="Search-first landing page — user intent mapping">
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 — DESIGN SOLUTION -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">04 &mdash; DESIGN SOLUTION</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">The Control Tower</h2>
      <p class="section-description">
        I designed a complete 2-year vision: a landing page that acts as a control tower,
        a mega menu that replaces the second sidebar, a hierarchy &amp; overrides hub,
        and consistent navigation that works across every app in the platform.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Layout structure -->
    <div class="gallery-two-col">
      <div class="gallery-item">
        <img src="/images/platform settings/SCR-20260406-obqy.png" alt="Complete layout structure — App Shell architecture">
        <div class="gallery-caption">
          <h4>Layout Architecture</h4>
          <p>App Shell &rarr; Global Header (Spaces) &rarr; Main Layout with full-width sheet component. The mega menu trigger replaces the second sidebar entirely.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gallery-item">
        <img src="/images/platform settings/SCR-20260406-obou.png" alt="Mega menu design decisions and rejected patterns">
        <div class="gallery-caption">
          <h4>Mega Menu Decisions</h4>
          <p>Single &ldquo;Settings&rdquo; trigger, click-only behavior (no hover), configurable per app. Rejected: 2-row tabs, simple dropdown, hover-to-open.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Inheritance model -->
    <div class="solution-gallery">
      <div class="gallery-item">
        <img src="/images/platform settings/SCR-20260406-obcw.png" alt="Settings inheritance model — Environment to Entity">
        <div class="gallery-caption">
          <h4>Inheritance Model</h4>
          <p>Environment-level defaults flow down to entities. The missing piece: users needed a mental model for how inheritance works AND a domain model for how schemas, objects, and entities relate.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Edge cases -->
    <div class="solution-gallery">
      <div class="gallery-item">
        <img src="/images/platform settings/SCR-20260406-obgc.png" alt="Inheritance edge cases — 7 scenarios with decision trees">
        <div class="gallery-caption">
          <h4>7 Inheritance Scenarios</h4>
          <p>I documented every edge case: ancestor resets, field changes, multi-entity impact, chain insertions, conflict resolution, scale operations, and batch resets. All designed from scratch&mdash;no prior documentation existed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 — THE MEGA MENU -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">05 &mdash; RECLAIMING SPACE</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Killing the Second Sidebar</h2>
      <p class="section-description">
        Every app in Dynatrace used a second sidebar for navigation. It consumed ~250px
        of screen width. Data showed most users were in India on small screens. Nobody was
        tackling this&mdash;until I started iterating.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="split-section">
      <div class="split-content">
        <h3>8 Iterations</h3>
        <p>
          It wasn&rsquo;t &ldquo;my task&rdquo;&mdash;or so my colleagues thought. But fixing the product
          experience IS the designer&rsquo;s task. I shared progress weekly, got feedback, iterated.
        </p>
        <p>
          By week three, the mood shifted from confusion to excitement. A principal designer
          from another focus area started meeting with me regularly&mdash;my mega menu would solve
          a complaint her users had been voicing for years.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mega menu is configurable per app. Settings gets categories (Data + Platform).
          The Spaces app gets &ldquo;Pick up where you left off&rdquo; + app grid. Each app defines
          what goes in the menu. The second sidebar dies.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight-card">
        <h4>The Consistency Trap</h4>
        <p>
          The design team of 70 designers was stuck in a common practice: not creating new
          components because a small design system team should handle &ldquo;consistency.&rdquo;
          But that team wasn&rsquo;t aware of every initiative and user need. What they released
          was bare minimum. The overall team simply reused these bare minimums, creating
          patterns that were strange for everyone outside Dynatrace.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top: 16px; color: #6c5ce7;">
          I started tutoring 4 designers in my focus area and one principal designer in another.
          The goal: understand that consistency is multi-dimensional, and that creating new
          patterns IS contributing to the design system.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 — IMPACT -->
  <div class="impact-section">
    <div class="impact-header">
      <h2 class="impact-title">Design Impact</h2>
      <p class="impact-subtitle">Reshaping platform configuration through design leadership</p>
    </div>
    <div class="impact-grid">
      <div class="impact-item">
        <span class="impact-label">Platforms analyzed in competitive research</span>
        <span class="impact-value">15+</span>
      </div>
      <div class="impact-item">
        <span class="impact-label">Mega menu iterations before team alignment</span>
        <span class="impact-value">8</span>
      </div>
      <div class="impact-item">
        <span class="impact-label">Inheritance edge cases documented</span>
        <span class="impact-value">7</span>
      </div>
      <div class="impact-item">
        <span class="impact-label">Filtering systems unified into one proposal</span>
        <span class="impact-value">3 &rarr; 1</span>
      </div>
      <div class="impact-item">
        <span class="impact-label">Designers mentored on design principles</span>
        <span class="impact-value">5</span>
      </div>
      <div class="impact-item">
        <span class="impact-label">Shipped: breadcrumbs, entity search, IA refactoring</span>
        <span class="impact-value">Shipped</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 07 — COMPLETE IA -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">07 &mdash; THE VISION</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Complete Information Architecture</h2>
      <p class="section-description">
        The full 2-year vision documented across multiple iterations&mdash;from landing page
        to category navigation, schema detail, overrides hub, and entity-level inheritance chains.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="research-image">
      <img src="/images/platform settings/SCR-20260406-obxw.png" alt="Complete information architecture documentation">
      <p class="image-caption">The complete IA: multiple iterations of category structures, view hierarchies, overrides hub design, and navigation patterns</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PROTOTYPE CTA -->
  <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 0;">
    <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(99,102,241,0.08), rgba(20,150,255,0.08)); border: 1px solid rgba(99,102,241,0.2); border-radius: 16px; padding: 40px 48px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 32px; flex-wrap: wrap;">
      <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 280px;">
        <span class="section-label" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Interactive Prototype</span>
        <h3 style="font-size: 22px; font-weight: 600; color: #ffffff; margin-top: 8px; margin-bottom: 8px;">Explore the Settings Prototype</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 15px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.6); line-height: 1.6; max-width: 520px;">Navigate the control tower landing page, category views, schema details, and overrides hub I designed and coded as a React prototype.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="https://spaces-prototype.netlify.app/settings" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; padding: 14px 28px; background: rgba(99,102,241,0.9); color: #ffffff; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; border-radius: 10px; text-decoration: none; white-space: nowrap; transition: background 0.2s ease;">
        View Prototype &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 08 — REFLECTION -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-number">08 &mdash; REFLECTION</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">Fixing What Nobody Would Touch</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="split-section">
      <div>
        <p class="section-description">
          This project taught me that the biggest design problems aren&rsquo;t in the interface&mdash;they&rsquo;re
          in how teams think about design. The word &ldquo;consistency&rdquo; had become a cage. Designers
          weren&rsquo;t exploring because exploring felt inconsistent. The design system team couldn&rsquo;t
          keep up because they didn&rsquo;t know what every initiative needed.
        </p>
        <p class="section-description" style="margin-top: 32px;">
          I worked on Spaces and Settings simultaneously for 6 months. The two initiatives are
          deeply connected&mdash;Spaces defines who can access what, Settings defines what they
          can configure. Designing both gave me a unique perspective: the governance layer and
          the configuration surface need to speak the same language.
        </p>
        <p class="section-description" style="margin-top: 32px;">
          Several features shipped before my departure: breadcrumbs that persist across app
          boundaries, the entity search pattern, and the IA refactoring was mid-implementation.
          The 2-year vision&mdash;the control tower landing page, the overrides hub, the mega
          menu&mdash;lives on in prototypes and documentation the team continues to reference.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight-card">
        <h4>What I Learned</h4>
        <p>
          <strong>Culture shapes products.</strong> The &ldquo;consistency trap&rdquo; wasn&rsquo;t a
          tooling problem&mdash;it was a mindset problem. Fixing it required mentoring, not Figma.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top: 16px;">
          <strong>Fix the product, not your task list.</strong> The mega menu wasn&rsquo;t in my brief.
          The filter unification wasn&rsquo;t in my brief. But they were the right things to do.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top: 16px;">
          <strong>Document everything.</strong> When I joined, there was no documentation from previous
          designers. I left behind edge case matrices, IA documents, competitive analysis, and
          interactive prototypes. The next designer won&rsquo;t start from zero.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

